#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
Repairs `arabicFootnotes`, which the first two passes never touched.

WHY. scripts/repair-quranic-letter-confusions.py and
scripts/complete-quranic-letter-confusions.py wrote only `arabicBody`. The
footnote field duplicates much of the body verbatim -- 206,448 of its words
align to a body passage -- so after those passes every lesson file carried a
stale second copy of its own text, and the footnote panel renders from it. A
reader saw the corrected word in the body and the corrupt one in the footnote
beside it. Only lessons 1-30 have footnotes at all; 31-56 are empty.

2,130 words, 2,237 letters, 30 lessons.

TWO SOURCES OF EVIDENCE, in this order.

1. THE REPAIRED BODY, 1,249 words. Where a footnote passage duplicates a body
   passage, the body reading has already been settled and checked, so it is the
   strongest warrant available. Located by folding away combining marks, the
   alif/hamza/wasla family and exactly the confusion classes the repairs worked
   in, then indexing every seven-word window of the body and looking each
   footnote word up in it. A word is repaired only where every locus that
   matches yields the same body reading; on an ambiguous match, nothing runs.
   617 of these stand where the body word is itself one of the 1,557 verified
   repairs. The other 632 stand where the body carries the Qur'anic form and the
   footnote does not, or where the body form is attested across the corpus and
   the footnote form is not.

2. THE WARSH TEXT, 865 words, for footnote passages with no body twin -- much of
   the apparatus is hadith citation, isnad and source references that appear
   nowhere in the commentary. Same method as the earlier passes: only inside a
   bracketed citation, only the same classes, settled by the aya or by a gloss
   beside it. Long spans go through a window alignment that requires a
   neighbouring word to align too; two- and three-word spans go through the
   uniqueness test, which accepts a span only where exactly one reading of the
   mushaf fits its folded form.

   Plus 16 divine names, on the rule scripts/repair-divine-names.py established
   for the body and never applied here: al-RAHMAL for al-Rahman, which is not a
   word, runs by rule; al-rajim for al-rahim is named site by site, because 23
   of the 31 occurrences in the footnotes are the refuge formula and correct.

THE BODY IS NOT ALWAYS RIGHT, so copying it can be a regression. Six places came
back where the footnote form was attested and the body form was not. Four were
the crude test misfiring -- `al-nabiyyin` (Q 4:69), `athaa idha afsada` glossing
Q 2:60, `barii'` and `bi-jaddika` are all right in the body -- and the footnote
is repaired there. Two are real, and neither footnote is touched:

  - Lesson 14, body @40318, reads `TA-ayyuha l-rasul` for Q 5:67 `YA-ayyuha
    l-rasul`. The footnote has it right. The ta/ya is inside the classes and the
    footnote is the witness, so this pass corrects the BODY there; it is the one
    body write in this file, and it is a defect of the earlier pass.
  - Lesson 11, body @4373, reads `la YA`MIRU ... wa-TA`MIRU` for Q 4:48 `la
    yaghfiru ... wa-yaghfiru`. The footnote's `yaghmiru` is nearer but also
    wrong. Getting from either to `yaghfiru` needs mim for fa, which is outside
    these classes. Both are left as they stand, for AK.

NOT REPAIRED, and read one by one out of 704 body-aligned and 872 Qur'an-matched
candidates: nine where the word is already right and the rule found it a rival
(`inna huwa illa nadhirun mubin` Q 29:50 offered as `anna`; `wa-ma rabbuka
bi-ghafilin `amma YA`MALUN` Q 6:132 offered as `ta`malun`; `alladhina YASUDDUN`
Q 11:19 offered as `tasuddun`; `wa-llahu `alimun bi-ma YA`MALUN` Q 12:19;
`tukhriju l-hayya` Q 3:27 offered as Q 6:95 `yukhriju`), one where the right
word is out of reach (`fa-akalahu l-dhi'b` Q 12:17 needs fa for ba, and the rule
offered `ya'kulahu`), and Q 6:51 SHAFI`, written `tabi`a`, which the second pass
had already had to revert in the body and which appears in the footnote too.

ANCHORING is as before: offsets into `arabicFootnotes`, the exact codepoints
expected there, one letter for one letter, asserts if a file has moved, and a
site already carrying the repaired word is skipped.

  python3 scripts/repair-footnote-letter-confusions.py          # dry run
  python3 scripts/repair-footnote-letter-confusions.py --write
"""
